<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
	Associated Domains, which is the iOS half of the deep link contract.

	READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING UNIVERSAL LINKS WORK. This file is INERT as it
	stands. Nothing in `App.xcodeproj` points at it yet, on purpose, and here is
	the reasoning: setting `CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS` by hand makes every build
	fail with "provisioning profile does not include the
	com.apple.developer.associated-domains entitlement" until the capability
	exists on the App ID, and the owner does not have an Apple Developer account
	yet. A repository that cannot be built is worse than one with a documented
	next step.

	THE OWNER'S THREE STEPS, in order. None can be done from inside this
	repository.

	1. Apple Developer portal, Certificates Identifiers and Profiles, the App ID
	   `com.vallospaces.app`. Tick Associated Domains, save, and let the provisioning
	   profiles regenerate.
	2. Xcode, the App target, Signing and Capabilities, the plus button,
	   Associated Domains. Xcode finds this file at `App/App.entitlements`,
	   adopts it rather than creating a second one, and sets the build setting
	   itself. The domain below should appear already filled in.
	3. Publish `apps/web/public/.well-known/apple-app-site-association` on the
	   live origin with the real Team ID in it. That file carries an obvious
	   placeholder today, `PLACEHOLDER_REPLACE_WITH_APPLE_TEAM_ID`, and the
	   whole `appIDs` entry has to read `<TeamID>.com.vallospaces.app`.

	   The Team ID is ten alphanumeric characters and there are two places to
	   read it. Apple Developer, Membership details, the field named Team ID.
	   Or in Xcode, the App target, Signing and Capabilities, where it is shown
	   in brackets beside the selected team. It is NOT the Apple ID email and
	   it is NOT the bundle identifier, which are the two things people paste
	   in by mistake. The placeholder is deliberately longer than ten
	   characters so that a file nobody filled in fails to match rather than
	   looking plausible.

	Then verify, because a silent failure here looks exactly like success:
	Apple caches the association file through its own CDN, so check what Apple
	actually has rather than what the server returns, at

	    https://app-site-association.cdn-apple.com/a/v1/vallospaces.com

	and expect a device to take up to a day to pick up a change. During
	development, the `applinks:` entries can be suffixed with
	`?mode=developer` to bypass the CDN, which is the only sane way to test a
	change to the file.

	The one domain is `www.vallospaces.com`, the canonical host, matching the
	Android manifest. The apex is NOT claimed: `vallospaces.com` answers every
	path, `/.well-known/apple-app-site-association` included, with a 308 to www
	(Vercel's domain setting), and Apple's CDN does not follow redirects, so an
	apex entry could never verify. The reasoning and the way back are in the
	App Links note in `AndroidManifest.xml` ("THE APEX").
-->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:www.vallospaces.com</string>
	</array>

	<!--
		PUSH NOTIFICATIONS. THE SAME THREE STEPS AND THE SAME MISSING ACCOUNT.

		`aps-environment` is the entitlement that lets a build register with
		APNs at all. Like the associated domains above it is INERT until the
		owner has an Apple Developer account, and for the identical reason:
		a build fails outright with "provisioning profile does not include the
		aps-environment entitlement" until the Push Notifications capability
		exists on the App ID `com.vallospaces.app`. It is written here so the
		value is ready and the step is recorded, not because it works today.

		THE OWNER'S STEPS FOR PUSH, in order, none doable from this repository.

		1. Apple Developer Program membership, 99 USD a year. There is no way
		   round this one: APNs has no free tier and no sandbox that works
		   without a team.
		2. Certificates Identifiers and Profiles, the App ID
		   `com.vallospaces.app`, tick Push Notifications, save, let the
		   profiles regenerate.
		3. Keys, plus, a new key with Apple Push Notifications service (APNs)
		   ticked. Download the `.p8`. IT DOWNLOADS EXACTLY ONCE and cannot be
		   retrieved again; a lost one has to be revoked and replaced. Then
		   set APNS_KEY_ID (the key's 10 character id), APNS_TEAM_ID (the same
		   Team ID the note above describes finding) and APNS_PRIVATE_KEY (the
		   whole .p8 contents) in the server environment. NOT in this
		   repository: see `src/lib/push/credentials.ts`.
		4. Xcode, the App target, Signing and Capabilities, plus, Push
		   Notifications. Xcode adopts this file rather than creating a second
		   one and sets `CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS` itself.

		THE VALUE BELOW IS `development` AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
		A build signed with a development profile receives a SANDBOX device
		token, and a sandbox token sent to the production gateway is refused
		400 BadDeviceToken, which reads exactly like a dead device and will
		have somebody retiring perfectly good tokens. `development` is what a
		local or TestFlight-from-Xcode build wants. AN APP STORE BUILD NEEDS
		THIS CHANGED TO `production`, and the server side switched with it by
		setting APNS_PRODUCTION=true. Xcode's automatic signing rewrites this
		for a Release archive, but the SERVER half does not change itself and
		is the half people forget.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>

	<!--
		SIGN IN WITH APPLE (STORE-02). Inert with the rest of this file until
		the App ID `com.vallospaces.app` has the Sign In with Apple capability
		ticked in the Apple Developer portal. The app only draws the button
		when Supabase reports the Apple provider enabled AND the running
		binary carries the `SignInWithApple` plugin, so this entitlement being
		present early changes nothing a person can see.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
